# The API is its own Node service, not part of the website deployment.
#
# Vercel builds every file under a top-level api/ directory as a serverless
# function, so without this the website deploy tried to compile the API's
# source, tests and build scripts as individual endpoints, and failed on the
# API's tsconfig asking for Node types. Anchored to the root so a Next.js
# app/api route would still deploy.
#
# The exceptions are the artifacts the docs pages read when the site builds:
# the spec behind the API reference (lib/docs/openapi.ts) and the five
# catalogue files behind the data and geography pages (lib/docs/catalogue.ts).
# Everything else the API serves from api/public stays out of the deployment.
#
# Directory contents are listed rather than the directories themselves
# because an ignored directory cannot have a file re-included from it: with
# `/api`, every `!` line below is silently ignored and the build fails with
# ENOENT. Vercel has no builder for .yaml or .json, so these deploy no extra
# functions. Adding a docs page that reads another api/ file means adding it
# here too.
/api/*
!/api/openapi.yaml
!/api/public
/api/public/*
!/api/public/data-catalog.json
!/api/public/export-manifest.json
!/api/public/boundary-releases.json
!/api/public/geography-inventory.json
!/api/public/map-resources.json
